{"summary": "`merge()` now does what its docstring says: intervals that touch get merged.

The bug was the check `s < out[-1][1]` in `intervals.py`. It only merged intervals that strictly overlapped, so `[[1, 3], [3, 4]]` (shared endpoint) and `[[1, 2], [3, 4]]` (no integer between them) stayed separate. I changed it to `s <= out[-1][1] + 1`.

I added tests to `test_intervals.py` for a shared endpoint, touching intervals, a real gap (stays separate), unsorted input with duplicates, and an empty list. `python -m unittest -v` ran 6 tests, all passing."}
